43
00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:17.280
Mark Blackman: at least two-thirds… at least a majority of the network attesting to the block to ensure a third of the nodes are not acting maliciously. So, very core to not only the security of the product, but also

44
00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:27.740
Mark Blackman: Users, smart contracts, mirror nodes, other downstream services, verifying the data coming out of the network.

45
00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:33.420
Mark Blackman: Has indeed been signed by a majority of the nodes on that network.

46
00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:36.150
Mark Blackman: So… Just want to kind of…

47
00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:42.520
Mark Blackman: At a high level, center you with what this is and why it's important.

48
00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:46.560
Mark Blackman: So let's look at how it's done today.

49
00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:50.809
Mark Blackman: So you'll see at the top, we're generating records.

50
00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:59.529
Mark Blackman: And using each… each node, will sign the hash of a record with an RSA signature.

51
00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:06.320
Mark Blackman: And what happens is every node provides a signature file, along with the record file.

52
00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:13.390
Mark Blackman: And so… These are uploaded, so in our cloud buckets, we have

53
00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:19.790
Mark Blackman: Not only records from every node on the network, but also their individual signature.

54
00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:28.650
Mark Blackman: And so, therefore, to verify a majority of the nodes have signed off on the exact same record.

55
00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:36.650
Mark Blackman: Mirror nodes will… pull multiple… Record files, multiple signature files.

56
00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:41.020
Mark Blackman: And really process and verify those signatures

57
00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:52.760
Mark Blackman: until they reach a number that is greater than majority of the network. So, basically, multiple files, multiple signatures have to be

58
00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:55.010
Mark Blackman: verified.

59
00:12:55.730 --> 00:13:03.699
Mark Blackman: To ensure that a majority of the nodes on the network have signed off on the same record file.

60
00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:07.490
Mark Blackman: So as you could imagine, this…

61
00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:15.990
Mark Blackman: Is slower, more costlier for verifiers of the network, But then also,

62
00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:23.830
Mark Blackman: You require multiple operations, multiple files, and really, there isn't one

63
00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:30.930
Mark Blackman: One proof that could be used to… to, verify any…

64
00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:37.989
Mark Blackman: piece of output of the network. And really, that is one of the major benefits of TSS.

65
00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:39.819
Mark Blackman: is… whoop.

66
00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:51.380
Mark Blackman: basically, what TSS allows us to do is to have a network signature So,

67
00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:56.780
Mark Blackman: The node still will sign and attest

68
00:13:56.910 --> 00:14:03.850
Mark Blackman: to the block coming out, this will be introduced in block streams. So the block of data coming out of the network

69
00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:15.579
Mark Blackman: However, they internally, share those signatures, and once that threshold of majority of the network is reached.

70
00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:21.490
Mark Blackman: Then, a single network signature is created, so therefore.

71
00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:30.270
Mark Blackman: Rather than having multiple node signatures that you have to evaluate, in the block, you'll have one block proof.

72
00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:40.790
Mark Blackman: And, with verifying that block proof, you could verify and prove that a majority of the network has signed off on that block.

73
00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:50.590
Mark Blackman: This will make blocks independently verifiable. You only need the data within a block and the block proof.

74
00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:57.339
Mark Blackman: to verify. So, from a mirror node and downstream component, side.

75
00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:02.520
Mark Blackman: It's… it's cost-efficient or cost-optimized compared to current.

76
00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:10.079
Mark Blackman: It's quicker, and then also, again, this block, I think Richard used the analogy.

77
00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:26.080
Mark Blackman: You could, print off a block and bury it in a yard, and then 100 years later, somebody could dig up that piece of data and actually verify that 10 years ago, all the majority of the nodes on the network.

78
00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:29.130
Mark Blackman: have attested and signed off on that block.

79
00:15:29.330 --> 00:15:34.220
Mark Blackman: So this basically provides an aggregated network signature

80
00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:42.080
Mark Blackman: With the threshold built in that a majority of the nodes have signed off on this block.

